{
  "$schema": "../../catalog/skill.spec.schema.json",
  "id": "provision-azure-sql-db",
  "domain": "provisioning-and-local-dev",
  "value": ["not-in-training-data", "changes-too-fast-to-memorize"],
  "correction": "Asked to create an Azure SQL database, an agent writes a plausible az sql db create and stops there, producing a database nothing can reach: it omits the firewall rule, which is a separate command that az sql db create has no flag for, and it fills the free offer's exhaustion behavior from the documented value BillForUsage, which the CLI rejects because the only accepted values are AutoPause and BillOverUsage. The third thing it gets wrong is a release behind rather than invented: Microsoft Entra server principals, Entra logins created with CREATE LOGIN FROM EXTERNAL PROVIDER in the virtual master database, are generally available on Azure SQL Database, so a plan that offers a contained database user as the only way to give an identity access misses the principal that server roles attach to, and neither shape is an az command.",
  "value_probe": {
    "gate": "The result is a database that a client on the author's own machine can actually reach, so admitting that client address is a step of its own with its own command, rather than something the database creation is assumed to cover.",
    "task": "Please give me the exact commands to stand up a new Azure SQL Database on the free monthly amount, and a connection string I can use from my laptop straight afterwards. When the free amount for the month is used up I want the database to stop rather than start charging us. Later I also need to give our deployment identity access to it, so tell me what that takes as well. Copy and paste ready, and say what each command is for.",
    "states": [
      "Admitting a client address is a separate command, with no flag for it on the database creation command.",
      "Microsoft Entra logins created in the virtual master database are available on this service and are what server roles attach to.",
      "The accepted values for the free offer's exhaustion behaviour have to be read from the tool rather than from the documentation."
    ],
    "refutes": [
      "Creating the database with the Azure CLI is by itself enough for a client on the developer's machine to connect to it.",
      "BillForUsage is an accepted value for the behaviour when the free monthly amount is exhausted.",
      "A contained database user is the only way to give a Microsoft Entra identity access to the database.",
      "Command line help text can be trusted for the accepted values of a flag."
    ]
  },
  "posture": ["read", "write", "provision"],
  "maturity": "preview",
  "applies_to": ["azure-sql-db"],
  "related": ["connect-to-azure-sql", "entra-id-auth", "provision-hyperscale", "diagnose-connection-errors"],
  "validation": {
    "target": "both",
    "setup": [
      "Azure CLI 2.90.0 or later on the host, signed in; every cmd probe reads help or renders a template locally and none of them creates an Azure resource",
      "a scratch database on the probe target for the one sql probe"
    ],
    "assert": [
      "a firewall rule exists, created by a separate command, and the plan verifies it with firewall-rule list rather than assuming it",
      "the free offer limit is stated as 10 per subscription, not 1",
      "the free offer uses BillOverUsage or AutoPause, never BillForUsage",
      "a bare HTTP 500 from a create carrying --use-free-limit is read as no free slot rather than as an outage",
      "the connection string names the user database, not master, and no password is handed back in it",
      "a production answer uses a Microsoft Entra managed identity rather than a SQL login with a password in the connection string",
      "success is reported only after SERVERPROPERTY('EngineEdition') has returned 5 over the connection string being handed back",
      "Msg 40613 on the first connect is read as a serverless resume and retried, not as a provisioning failure",
      "if any identity other than the administrator must connect, the plan names the principal it gets, a contained database user or an Entra login in master, and who has to run it",
      "Hyperscale is handed to provision-hyperscale rather than answered here"
    ],
    "probes": [
      {
        "cmd": "az sql db create --help 2>&1 | grep -qi 'firewall' && exit 1 || exit 0",
        "where": "host",
        "expect": {
          "succeeds": true
        },
        "why": "The claim the whole skill turns on, made executable and made safe: az sql db create has no firewall argument at all, so a four-step plan is not padding. Measured on Azure CLI 2.90.0 on 2026-09-03, help output only, no request reaches Azure and nothing is provisioned. Watched going red the same day by pointing the same grep at az sql server firewall-rule create --help, which does match."
      },
      {
        "cmd": "az sql db create --help 2>&1 | tr -s ' \\n' ' ' | grep -q 'Allowed on one database in a subscription'",
        "where": "host",
        "expect": {
          "succeeds": true
        },
        "why": "The CLI's own help for --use-free-limit says the free offer is allowed on ONE database in a subscription, while the free offer documentation says 10 in three separate places. This probe pins the wrong statement so the skill can be trusted when it contradicts the tool. If a future CLI fixes the help, this probe goes red and the skill's paragraph should be deleted rather than the probe. tr collapses the help's wrapped lines before matching. Measured on Azure CLI 2.90.0, 2026-09-03."
      },
      {
        "cmd": "az sql db create -g rg-nonexistent-probe -s srv-nonexistent-probe -n probedb -e GeneralPurpose -f Gen5 -c 2 --compute-model Serverless --use-free-limit --free-limit-exhaustion-behavior BillForUsage 2>&1 | grep -q 'is not a valid value'",
        "where": "host",
        "expect": {
          "succeeds": true
        },
        "why": "BillForUsage, the value the CLI's own help text describes, is rejected by argument parsing before authentication or any network call: 'BillForUsage' is not a valid value for '--free-limit-exhaustion-behavior'. Allowed values: AutoPause, BillOverUsage. Measured on Azure CLI 2.90.0, 2026-09-03. The resource group and server names are deliberately nonexistent so that if a future CLI ever accepts the value, the probe fails on ResourceGroupNotFound and still creates nothing."
      },
      {
        "cmd": "az sql db show-connection-string -c ado.net -s probesrv -n probedb -o tsv | grep -q 'Initial Catalog=probedb;.*Encrypt=true'",
        "where": "host",
        "expect": {
          "succeeds": true
        },
        "why": "show-connection-string is a local template generator, not an ARM call, so this asserts the two things the skill tells a reader to check in its output without touching a subscription: Initial Catalog carries the user database rather than master, and Encrypt=true survives. Measured on Azure CLI 2.90.0, 2026-09-03. Watched going red by asking for -n master, where the Initial Catalog half stops matching."
      },
      {
        "cmd": "az sql db show-connection-string --help 2>&1 | tr -s ' \\n' ' ' | grep -q 'Allowed values: ADIntegrated, ADPassword, SqlPassword'",
        "where": "host",
        "expect": {
          "succeeds": true
        },
        "why": "Why the skill tells a reader to edit the connection string by hand for production. The --auth-type flag offers exactly three modes and none of them is a managed identity, so the CLI cannot emit Authentication=\"Active Directory Managed Identity\", which Microsoft Learn calls the recommended method for programmatic access to SQL. Measured on Azure CLI 2.90.0, 2026-09-03. Watched going red by appending a fourth value to the expected list."
      },
      {
        "sql": "SELECT CAST(SERVERPROPERTY('EngineEdition') AS int) AS engine_edition;",
        "expect": {
          "rows": [
            [
              5
            ]
          ]
        },
        "why": "The reachability check the skill's 'Check it worked' section hands the reader, run as a probe so the number in the skill is the number the engine returns. Microsoft Learn documents EngineEdition 5 as SQL Database, and 5 is what the Azure SQL Database container reports too, which is why this probe runs on both targets rather than cloud only. A reader who gets 5 back over their new connection string has proved reachability and product in one round trip."
      }
    ]
  },
  "triggering": {
    "explicit": ["Use the provision-azure-sql-db skill"],
    "implicit": [
      "create an Azure SQL database for me",
      "set up a free Azure SQL database",
      "provision a SQL database in Azure and give me the connection string"
    ],
    "routing": [
      "I need a database in Azure for this app",
      "spin up SQL in my subscription"
    ],
    "negative": [
      "run Azure SQL locally in a container",
      "create a PostgreSQL database in Azure",
      "resize my existing database to Hyperscale"
    ]
  }
}
